==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
Cognate with English {{term|cheap|lang=en}}, German {{term|kaufen|lang=de}}. Common Germanic of debated etymology: 1. {{proto|Germanic|kaupaz|pos=n|lang=non|title=Common Germanic}}, from {{etyl|la|non}} {{term|caupo|caupō|tradesman|lang=la}}. Cognate with {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍀𐍉𐌽||barter|tr=kaupōn}}. 2. Of native origin, going on {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽||strike, cuff|tr=kaupatjan|lang=got}}, which is morphologically compatible with ''kaupa''. Senses in other languages derived by relating striking (of hands) to transaction, as with {{term|strike a bargain|lang=en}}, ''{{soplink|slá|kaup|form2=kaupi|lang=non}}''.<ref>George Hempl, University of Michigan. "Etymologies - Cheap, cope, coup, kaupatjan, caupo, κάπηλος, etc." ''Modern language notes, Volume 17,'' pp. 210-212. Johns Hopkins University, JSTOR, via [http://books.google.com/books?id=hfwmAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA210 Google Books].</ref>

